//? Title: Reconstruction Anomaly: What the Residual Misses
//? Subtitle: Frozen autoencoder, explicit nominal thresholds and paired corruption controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A frozen 784–32–784 MNIST autoencoder supplies per-image squared reconstruction residuals.
Three scoring rules are calibrated separately at the nearest-rank 95th percentile of 200
nominal validation scores, with a strict greater-than flag. All ten digit classes are nominal.
The held-out 200 images receive three predetermined transformations, retaining all 600
paired synthetic cases. Autoencoder residuals detect every white-patch case and 99.5% of
right shifts, but only 2% of darkened images; darkening AUROC is .4028. A simple mean-ink
rule has different strengths and misses. Nominal held-out false-positive rates are 9%,7%,6%
for autoencoder, fitting-mean and ink scores, versus 5% on calibration. A complete native
prerequisite shows all 96 fitting updates; the application adds zero. Validation reuse,
prevalence, paired sources and the separate blank control remain explicit.
)
\toc

> A score, a calibration rule and an evaluation pool have different jobs

The encoder was fitted to 1,000 nominal images; every digit class is nominal. Its fixed
reconstruction is compared with the observed input, not with a clean target available at
deployment. A fitting-only mean image supplies a nonlearned residual baseline. An absolute
mean-ink deviation supplies a simple brightness baseline. Each rule gets its own threshold
from the same 200 clean validation images. No transformed cases or held-out test outcomes
select those thresholds.

The prerequisite already uses these validation images for encoder checkpoint selection.
They are reused for threshold calibration, rather than reserved as a fresh calibration pool.
The resulting 5% calibration false-positive rate is an empirical order-statistic result;
there is no independent-holdout or distribution-free deployment guarantee. The 200 test
images remain held out from model/threshold selection.

