> A hand-checkable residual

For a sample with D coordinates, the score is the feature-order sum of squared differences,
divided by D. Inputs widen to double before subtraction. Rows [0,1] and [2,3], reconstructed
as [0,0], score (0²+1²)/2=.5 and (2²+3²)/2=6.5. The score is not the squared difference
of row means, a batch mean or a clipped-pixel loss. These diagnostic functions deliberately
stop gradients and leave caller flags unchanged; the prerequisite uses a separate training loss.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/anomaly_toy_residual.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n         = dm::nn;
  const auto original = n::tensor({0., 1., 2., 3.}, {2, 2}), reconstructed = n::zeros({2, 2});
  const auto scores = n::models::ReconstructionScores(original, reconstructed);
  std::print(
      "Two per-case mean squared residuals: {:.1f}, {:.1f}.\n", scores.at({0}), scores.at({1}));
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.points({0, 2}, {1, 3}, "Original rows", {37, 99, 235}, 5.)
      .points({0}, {0}, "Reconstruction", {220, 38, 38}, 5.)
      .lines({0, 0}, {0, 1}, "", {100, 116, 139}, 1.5)
      .lines({0, 2}, {0, 3}, "", {100, 116, 139}, 1.5)
      .x_limits(-.4, 2.6)
      .y_limits(-.4, 4.3)
      .x_label("Coordinate0")
      .y_label("Coordinate1")
      .title("Per-case residual: squared displacement divided by two")
      .save_svg("build/anomaly_toy_residual.svg");
}
```
