> Nearest rank and strict inequality can be checked separately

Sorting N nominal scores and choosing one-based rank ceil(.95*N) gives an existing score;
there is no interpolation. Scores 0,...,19 give rank19, threshold18. A score equal to18 is
unflagged;19 is flagged. The API uses the explicit double product without a rounding fuzz.
This is the inverse empirical-CDF nearest-rank convention, related to
[R quantile type1](https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/quantile.html),
with an explicit positive quantile and no fuzz rather than R's default interpolation.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/anomaly_toy_threshold.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace n            = dm::nn;
  const auto   scores    = n::arange(0, 20);
  const double threshold = n::models::NominalThreshold(scores);
  const auto   flags     = n::greater(scores, threshold);
  std::print(
      "Threshold {:.0f}; strict flags {}/20. Value18 stays unflagged.\n",
      threshold,
      n::count_nonzero(flags).item<std::int64_t>());
  auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.points(
       n::arange(1, 21).to_vector<double>(),
       scores.to_vector<double>(),
       "Nominal scores",
       {37, 99, 235},
       4.)
      .lines({0, 21}, {threshold, threshold}, "Threshold18", {100, 116, 139}, 1.5)
      .points({20}, {19}, "Strict flag", {220, 38, 38}, 5.)
      .x_limits(0, 21)
      .y_limits(-1, 27)
      .x_label("Sorted nominal rank")
      .y_label("Score")
      .title("Nearest-rank threshold ties are unflagged")
      .save_svg("build/anomaly_toy_threshold.svg");
}
```
